 - Avoid depending on builtins that might be shadowed or restricted in the grader's environment. For hot-path primitives (modular exponentiation, gcd, etc.), consider writing small helper functions instead of relying on builtins whose behavior could differ. For example, write a manual square-and-multiply modular exponentiation instead of the 3-argument `pow` when the environment is uncertain.
 - Include all imports you use explicitly (e.g. `from typing import List`), and do not shadow builtins with your own variable or function names (e.g. `list`, `sum`, `max`, `min`, `pow`, `input`, `len`, `str`).
 - Keep the solution correct for the full constraints, not only for the examples: consider the time and memory complexity needed for the largest inputs.
